-- Spawned subprocess is blocked --

Issue:
When user.input(...) is requested the RUN command is blocked by the user.input(...) if the subprocess is spawned like this
result = subprocess.run(['python', temp_file_path], capture_output=True, text=True)

How to reproduce:
user.input(...) loop running, then ask agent to perform python hello world run task
The user.input(...) will block execution of the hello world program

Fix:
result = subprocess.run(['python', temp_file_path], text=True, stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE)

Background:
When you call subprocess they inherit the I/O streams of the process that spawned them by default. That is the case when you run the process
as above in the Issue section. The user.input(...) has the lock on the stdin stream so the child process has to wait to acquire that stream before it can launch.
In the subprocess run in the fix section, passing subprocess.PIPE is a flag argument that tells subprocess.run to create its own stdin/stdout/stderr objects instead
of inheriting from the parent process.
